We have that
\begin{align*}
\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i &= 96, \\
\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^2 &= 144, \\
\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^3 &= 216.
\end{align*}
Let $S_1 = \sum_{i = 1}^n a_i, S_2 = \sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^2, S_3 = \sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^3.$ Then we have
\begin{align*}
S_1 &= 96, \\
S_2 &= 144, \\
S_3 &= 216.
\end{align*}
We can use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality to get
\begin{align*}
S_1^2 &\ge S_2 S_3, \\
96^2 &\ge 144 \cdot 216, \\
3024 &\ge 3024.
\end{align*}
This is an equality, so the equality case of Cauchy-Schwarz must hold. This means that the vectors $(a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n)$ and $(a_1^2, a_2^2, \dots, a_n^2)$ are proportional. Thus, there exists a constant $k$ such that
\begin{align*}
a_i &= k a_i^2, \\
a_i^2 &= k a_i^3.
\end{align*}
Then we have
\begin{align*}
\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i &= k \sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^2 = 96, \\
\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^3 &= k \sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^3 = 216.
\end{align*}
Thus, $k = \frac{96}{216} = \frac{4}{9}$. Then we have
\begin{align*}
a_i &= \frac{4}{9} a_i^2, \\
a_i^2 &= \frac{4}{9} a_i^3.
\end{align*}
Then we have
\begin{align*}
\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^2 &= \frac{4}{9} \sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^3 = 144, \\
\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^3 &= \frac{4}{9} \sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^2 = 216.
\end{align*}
Thus, $n = \frac{144}{216} = \frac{2}{3}$. Thus, the sum of all possible values of $n$ is $\boxed{2}$.
Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{2}$. I hope it is correct.
